502
00:29:56,000 --> 00:30:05,000
And the CT, the angiocoronary CT was the right one because this is a retro aortic anomalous coronary.

503
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,000
And this is the famous RAC sign.

504
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:18,000
You can see in the echo in the 4-chamber view that you have this tubular structure with some hyperechoic parallel walls.

505
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:23,000
And this runs behind the aorta, so very close to the AV groove.

506
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
And this is very typical.

507
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:29,000
Sometimes you can see it also in the parasternal long axis view.

508
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:34,000
And so the suspicion that raises is of a retro aortic anomalous coronary.

509
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:39,000
Usually it is a circumflex arising from the right coronary sinus.

510
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:43,000
So CT is supposed to be the best technique for looking for that.

511
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:45,000
Although the RAC sign is interesting.

512
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:51,000
